encyclopedia :enuli do
  name "Enuli"
  summary "Enuli are blind water animals that perceive living electrical activity, stressed stone and moving water, sometimes sharing those senses through contact."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :animal
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :resonance, :subject_hab_life
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when all: { place: [:sealed_hab, :waterway] }
  biology "Soft-bodied swimmers with a broad contact surface beneath the head and rows of pressure-sensitive folds"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "Smooth pale bodies have no visible eyes; the head widens into a flexible pad.", behavior: "Rest against warm bodies, graze submerged growth and retreat through narrow water passages when alarmed.", threat: "A shared sensory journey can become overwhelming when an animal panics or enters a densely active space.", senses: "Electrical activity, pressure and stress carried through wet stone form a continuous nonvisual environment."
  prose <<~PROSE
    Enuli navigate by electrical activity in living bodies and by pressure and strain transmitted through water and stone. They have no useful sight. A person's face contributes little to their recognition, while roots, muscles and moving water stand out in patterns a human observer normally misses.

    The population at #{ref :selk} admits some people resting against an animal to the experience of another enuli beneath the hab. Keepers recognize the same responses within separated groups. #{ref :seren} holds their observations, including occasions when contact produced no shared perception. Transporting an animal has not made the experience reliably available wherever its pool is placed.

    A person sharing an animal's senses can encourage movement through remembered sensations and bodily inclinations. An instruction expressed as words supplies little direction. Hunger, familiar feeding places and immediate alarm retain their force. Keepers end a difficult contact by gently separating the resting animal from the pilgrim rather than startling it into flight.
  PROSE
  cue "A blind swimmer rests its broad head against a forearm and follows the pulse beneath the skin."
  prose <<~PROSE
    #{encyclopedia_ref :enuli_companion, "Enuli companions"} learn individuals' feeding preferences as well as people's descriptions. A swimmer that repeatedly turns toward a submerged root may be seeking food there while its human partner reports the same approach as an irresistible attraction. Keepers at #{ref :selk} compare journeys before and after ordinary feeding. The differences help them recognize hunger without assuming that every unfamiliar sensation has the same cause.
  PROSE
  cue "The pilgrim describes a familiar orchard as branching currents, with moving knots where people stand."
  affordance "Shared perception can reveal water and stressed stone behind surfaces that instruments cannot easily reach."
  variation "Individuals favor different feeding grounds and develop recognizable turns through narrow passages."
  variation "Separated groups vary in whether contact produces any shared perception at all."
end
